## Two distributions, one decision



:::: {.r-stack}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](01_Intro_bmm_SDT_files/figure-revealjs/sdt-dist-1.png){fig-alt='Two overlapping evidence distributions, one for new items and one for old items shifted to the right.' width=960}
:::
:::


::: {.fragment fragment-index=1}

::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](01_Intro_bmm_SDT_files/figure-revealjs/sdt-dprime-1.png){fig-alt='The same two evidence distributions with a double-headed arrow between their means, labeled d-prime.' width=960}
:::
:::

:::

::: {.fragment fragment-index=2}

::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](01_Intro_bmm_SDT_files/figure-revealjs/sdt-crit-1.png){fig-alt='The two evidence distributions with d-prime marked and a criterion line; the areas right of the criterion are shaded and labeled false alarms and hits.' width=960}
:::
:::

:::

::::

::: {.fragment fragment-index=1}
- $d'$: separation of the two distributions
:::

::: {.fragment fragment-index=2}
- $c$: how much evidence it takes to say "old", counted from the new-item mean
:::

::: {.notes}
Three steps. First the two distributions: memory strength for new and for
old items. Click: d', the distance between them. Click: the criterion. Every
item to the right of it gets an "old" response, so old items right of the
criterion are hits and new items right of it are false alarms. Two numbers
describe the observer: separation and threshold. The threshold is measured
from the mean of the new-item distribution, which is where bmm puts it too:
c = 0 is a criterion right at the new-item mean. The next slide moves the
threshold.
:::

## Moving the criterion changes accuracy {.smaller}

Assume two people with the same memory strength ($d' = 1.3$) but different
criteria. $c$ counts from the new-item mean, so $c = d'/2 = 0.65$ sits halfway
between the distributions; a lower $c$ says "old" more readily:



:::: {.columns}

::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.fragment}

::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](01_Intro_bmm_SDT_files/figure-revealjs/crit-a-1.png){fig-alt='New-item and old-item evidence distributions with the criterion halfway between them; the areas right of the criterion are shaded.' width=528}
:::
:::

:::

::: {.fragment style="text-align: center;"}
Hits .74 · False alarms .26 · Accuracy **.74**
:::
:::

::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.fragment}

::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](01_Intro_bmm_SDT_files/figure-revealjs/crit-b-1.png){fig-alt='The same two distributions with the criterion shifted left to the new-item mean; larger areas right of the criterion are shaded for both item types.' width=528}
:::
:::

:::

::: {.fragment style="text-align: center;"}
Hits .91 · False alarms .52 · Accuracy **.70**
:::
:::

::::

::: {.notes}
d' is 1.3 for both people, so their memory is identical. Person A has a
neutral criterion, halfway between the distributions (c = 0.65, half of d').
Person B has a liberal criterion (c = −0.05, right at the new-item mean):
hits and false alarms both go up, and accuracy drops. Everything an accuracy analysis sees has changed, although memory has
not. The real data will show the same pattern.
:::

## The data: recognition memory {.smaller}



Data from Koen, Aly, Wang, and Yonelinas (2013), available in the MPTinR
package:

::: {.incremental}
- Participants studied word lists, then made old/new judgments on a **6-point
  confidence scale** (1 = *sure new* … 6 = *sure old*)
- Two **between-subjects** test conditions with 48 participants
  each:
  - `full_attention`: test under full attention (Exp. 2)
  - `immediate`: test immediately after study (Exp. 4)
- Old/new response: ratings 4 to 6 count as "old"
- 23,902 trials in total; codebook in
